<p>President Trump said on Saturday that &quot;many countries&quot; would join the United States in sending warships to keep the Strait of Hormuz open, after Iran&#x27;s new supreme leader Ayatollah Mojtaba Khamenei said the waterway should stay closed to pressure Washington. Trump said he hoped China, France, Japan, South Korea and the UK would take part, though the White House did not immediately identify any governments that had agreed. The appeal came as the conflict entered its third week, with Tehran threatening broader retaliation after US strikes on Iran&#x27;s Kharg Island oil facilities and shipping through the strait still heavily disrupted. Because the Strait of Hormuz is a critical route for Gulf energy exports, the standoff has raised fears of a longer oil supply shock and wider regional escalation, while also sharpening calls for countries that depend on the passage to share more of the burden</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/RSQSYNLAQFSWkTvDVwkbp_VB6tb4i6wTxuFKt0UEZ7b4ngvqgduRuzo9HzHlSNqMghxy856jAd_b7oTNMPecSghWI47f_OpBBiQeFKPmOSCQ' alt='US President Donald Trump speaks to the media before boarding Air Force One at Joint Base Andrews, Maryland, the US on March 13.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>UK stance: The Financial Times reported that London declined to send an aircraft carrier to the Gulf despite President Trump&#x27;s public appeal for allied naval support.</li><li>Oil shock: Global oil prices have jumped about 40% since Iran choked off the passage and attacked Gulf energy facilities, according to Al-Monitor&#x27;s reporting.</li><li>Regional threat: Iran warned it could target facilities across the region with US ties, while The Hindu reported evacuation warnings around three major ports in the United Arab Emirates.</li><li>US posture: Trump had earlier said the US Navy would &quot;very soon&quot; begin escorting tankers through the strait before broadening the effort into a call for international support.</li></ul><blockquote>Many Countries, especially those who are affected by Iran’s attempted closure of the Hormuz Strait, will be sending War Ships, in conjunction with the United States of America, to keep the Strait open and safe - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: President Trump said countries that depend on Gulf oil should help secure the waterway and publicly named China, France, Japan, South Korea and the UK as partners he wanted involved. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd039n9vj3vo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Ayatollah Mojtaba Khamenei: Iran&#x27;s new supreme leader said blocking the Strait of Hormuz should serve as leverage against Washington, framing the closure as a pressure tactic rather than a temporary military move. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982096/trump-claims-many-countries-will-send-warships-to-keep-strait-of-hormuz-open'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>UK government: The Financial Times reported that London declined to send an aircraft carrier to the Gulf even as Washington pressed allies to contribute naval forces. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/d1b23f3e-a976-4ff1-a653-4155e7a8695e'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Iranian authorities: Iran signaled it would step up retaliation after US strikes, including threats against regional sites linked to the United States. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/trump-calls-allies-help-secure-strait-hormuz-iran-vows-step-retaliation'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982096/trump-claims-many-countries-will-send-warships-to-keep-strait-of-hormuz-open'>Trump claims ‘many countries’ will send warships to keep Strait of Hormuz open</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/4dd2173b-b283-41e4-8dee-4f04919dea68'>Trump urges China and UK to send warships to reopen Strait of Hormuz</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd039n9vj3vo'>Trump urges UK and other nations to send 